1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,829
Two million years ago, our ancestors were

2
00:00:02,829 --> 00:00:05,399
not hunters. They were often the hunted.

3
00:00:06,049 --> 00:00:08,401
Leopards dragged them into caves; their

4
00:00:08,401 --> 00:00:10,753
bones have been found with tooth marks.

5
00:00:11,403 --> 00:00:13,277
Today, there are about eight billion of

6
00:00:13,277 --> 00:00:13,545
us.

7
00:00:14,195 --> 00:00:16,471
We live on every continent, we've walked

8
00:00:16,471 --> 00:00:18,800
on the Moon, and we've changed the planet

9
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:19,329
itself.

10
00:00:19,979 --> 00:00:21,604
So here's a strange fact:

11
00:00:21,604 --> 00:00:24,099
according to scientists, we are not at

12
00:00:24,099 --> 00:00:25,782
the top of the food chain.

13
00:00:26,432 --> 00:00:29,197
In 2013, a team of researchers calculated

14
00:00:29,197 --> 00:00:32,026
humanity's average "trophic level" — where

15
00:00:32,026 --> 00:00:34,477
we sit in the food chain — from what

16
00:00:34,477 --> 00:00:36,991
people actually eat around the world.

17
00:00:37,641 --> 00:00:39,437
The answer: about 2.2.

18
00:00:40,087 --> 00:00:42,119
That's roughly the same as an anchovy.

19
00:00:42,769 --> 00:00:45,177
A polar bear or a killer whale sits near

20
00:00:45,177 --> 00:00:45,457
5.

21
00:00:46,107 --> 00:00:48,069
We're not the strongest predator.

22
00:00:48,069 --> 00:00:49,376
We're not even close.

23
00:00:50,026 --> 00:00:51,424
And yet we rule the planet.

24
00:00:52,074 --> 00:00:52,636
How?

25
00:00:53,286 --> 00:00:55,858
If you like big questions about who we are

26
00:00:55,858 --> 00:00:57,914
and how we got here, subscribe.

27
00:00:57,914 --> 00:00:59,857
It genuinely helps the channel.

28
00:01:00,507 --> 00:01:02,844
Look at the human body as a zoologist

29
00:01:02,844 --> 00:01:03,370
would.

30
00:01:04,020 --> 00:01:06,253
No claws. Small teeth.

31
00:01:06,253 --> 00:01:07,414
Thin skin.

32
00:01:08,064 --> 00:01:09,476
We're slow sprinters:

33
00:01:09,476 --> 00:01:12,064
a rabbit can outrun the fastest person on

34
00:01:12,064 --> 00:01:12,594
Earth.

35
00:01:13,244 --> 00:01:15,739
Pound for pound, a chimpanzee's muscles

36
00:01:15,739 --> 00:01:18,114
are roughly one and a half times more

37
00:01:18,114 --> 00:01:19,421
powerful than ours.

38
00:01:20,071 --> 00:01:21,957
And our babies are almost uniquely

39
00:01:21,957 --> 00:01:22,569
helpless.

40
00:01:23,219 --> 00:01:25,813
A baby giraffe can stand within an hour.

41
00:01:25,813 --> 00:01:28,407
A human baby takes about a year to walk.

42
00:01:29,057 --> 00:01:31,629
On paper, we're one of the worst-designed

43
00:01:31,629 --> 00:01:33,500
animals to dominate a planet.

44
00:01:34,150 --> 00:01:35,645
The first secret was fire.

45
00:01:36,295 --> 00:01:38,743
Our ancestors were using fire at least

46
00:01:38,743 --> 00:01:41,251
hundreds of thousands of years ago, and

47
00:01:41,251 --> 00:01:42,744
possibly much earlier.

48
00:01:43,394 --> 00:01:45,634
Fire gave light, warmth, and protection

49
00:01:45,634 --> 00:01:46,595
from predators.

50
00:01:47,245 --> 00:01:49,021
But its biggest gift was cooking.

51
00:01:49,671 --> 00:01:52,330
Harvard anthropologist Richard Wrangham

52
00:01:52,330 --> 00:01:54,867
argues that cooking changed our bodies.

53
00:01:55,517 --> 00:01:57,798
Cooked food is softer and gives more

54
00:01:57,798 --> 00:02:00,195
energy for less chewing and digestion.

55
00:02:00,845 --> 00:02:03,119
And that energy went somewhere expensive:

56
00:02:03,119 --> 00:02:03,894
the brain.

57
00:02:04,544 --> 00:02:07,057
Your brain is about 2% of your body

58
00:02:07,057 --> 00:02:09,835
weight, but it uses roughly 20% of your

59
00:02:09,835 --> 00:02:10,496
energy.

60
00:02:11,146 --> 00:02:13,599
A big brain is a luxury, and cooking may

61
00:02:13,599 --> 00:02:15,196
have helped us afford it.

62
00:02:15,846 --> 00:02:18,067
In a sense, we didn't just tame fire.

63
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:19,677
Fire helped make us human.

64
00:02:20,327 --> 00:02:22,694
The second secret is hiding in your legs.

65
00:02:23,344 --> 00:02:25,545
We're bad sprinters, but we're incredible

66
00:02:25,545 --> 00:02:26,895
long-distance runners.

67
00:02:27,545 --> 00:02:29,997
We sweat to cool down, we have long legs

68
00:02:29,997 --> 00:02:31,821
and springy Achilles tendons,

69
00:02:32,471 --> 00:02:34,875
and we can keep running in the heat long

70
00:02:34,875 --> 00:02:36,832
after most animals have to stop.

71
00:02:37,482 --> 00:02:39,861
Scientists Daniel Lieberman and Dennis

72
00:02:39,861 --> 00:02:42,186
Bramble argue this allowed persistence

73
00:02:42,186 --> 00:02:42,835
hunting:

74
00:02:43,485 --> 00:02:46,161
chasing an animal in the midday heat, for

75
00:02:46,161 --> 00:02:48,898
hours, until it collapses from exhaustion.

76
00:02:49,548 --> 00:02:52,029
Some hunters in the Kalahari have been

77
00:02:52,029 --> 00:02:54,570
documented doing exactly this in recent

78
00:02:54,570 --> 00:02:55,115
times.

79
00:02:55,765 --> 00:02:56,859
And then there's throwing.

80
00:02:57,509 --> 00:03:00,093
Humans are the only animals that can throw

81
00:03:00,093 --> 00:03:01,414
hard and accurately.

82
00:03:02,064 --> 00:03:04,828
A 2013 study found that changes in our

83
00:03:04,828 --> 00:03:07,660
shoulders, appearing around two million

84
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:10,020
years ago, work like a catapult,

85
00:03:10,670 --> 00:03:13,263
storing energy and releasing it in a snap.

86
00:03:13,913 --> 00:03:15,523
We didn't need to be strong.

87
00:03:15,523 --> 00:03:17,652
We could tire our prey out, and hit it

88
00:03:17,652 --> 00:03:18,639
from a distance.

89
00:03:19,289 --> 00:03:21,584
But fire, running and throwing still don't

90
00:03:21,584 --> 00:03:22,707
explain everything.

91
00:03:23,357 --> 00:03:25,254
Other species have had their own

92
00:03:25,254 --> 00:03:27,531
superpowers. None of them built cities.

93
00:03:28,181 --> 00:03:30,800
The real secret, many researchers argue,

94
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:33,480
is something you can't see in a skeleton:

95
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:35,829
we learn from each other, and the

96
00:03:35,829 --> 00:03:36,840
knowledge adds up.

97
00:03:37,490 --> 00:03:38,969
Here's a story that shows it.

98
00:03:39,619 --> 00:03:43,024
In 1860, the explorers Burke and Wills

99
00:03:43,024 --> 00:03:46,278
set out to cross Australia from south to

100
00:03:46,278 --> 00:03:46,959
north.

101
00:03:47,609 --> 00:03:49,639
They were well funded and well equipped.

102
00:03:50,289 --> 00:03:52,163
On the way back, they ran out of food.

103
00:03:52,813 --> 00:03:54,499
They found a plant called nardoo

104
00:03:55,149 --> 00:03:57,767
and learned from local Aboriginal people

105
00:03:57,767 --> 00:04:00,020
how to grind its seeds into flour.

106
00:04:00,670 --> 00:04:02,008
They ate it every day.

107
00:04:02,008 --> 00:04:03,720
And they kept getting weaker.

108
00:04:04,370 --> 00:04:07,134
What they didn't know was that nardoo,

109
00:04:07,134 --> 00:04:10,167
prepared the wrong way, contains an enzyme

110
00:04:10,167 --> 00:04:12,054
that destroys vitamin B1.

111
00:04:12,704 --> 00:04:15,281
The local Yandruwandha people prepared

112
00:04:15,281 --> 00:04:17,858
it with careful steps, using water, that

113
00:04:17,858 --> 00:04:18,817
made it safe.

114
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:21,218
Burke and Wills skipped those steps.

115
00:04:21,868 --> 00:04:22,923
Both men died.

116
00:04:23,573 --> 00:04:25,928
One of their companions survived — because

117
00:04:25,928 --> 00:04:28,231
the Yandruwandha took him in and fed him.

118
00:04:28,881 --> 00:04:31,587
Intelligent individuals, alone, could not

119
00:04:31,587 --> 00:04:33,925
survive where an entire culture had

120
00:04:33,925 --> 00:04:35,340
learned how to live.

121
00:04:35,990 --> 00:04:38,742
Anthropologist Joseph Henrich calls this

122
00:04:38,742 --> 00:04:41,372
cumulative culture, and he argues it's

123
00:04:41,372 --> 00:04:43,145
the secret of our success.

124
00:04:43,795 --> 00:04:46,235
No single person invented the bow, the

125
00:04:46,235 --> 00:04:47,902
canoe, or the smartphone.

126
00:04:48,552 --> 00:04:51,148
Each generation inherits what the last one

127
00:04:51,148 --> 00:04:53,686
figured out, adds a little, and passes it

128
00:04:53,686 --> 00:04:54,032
on.

129
00:04:54,682 --> 00:04:57,098
Over thousands of years, a ladder of

130
00:04:57,098 --> 00:04:59,762
knowledge grows that no individual could

131
00:04:59,762 --> 00:05:01,001
ever climb alone.

132
00:05:01,651 --> 00:05:03,178
And we're built for this.

133
00:05:03,178 --> 00:05:04,924
Children are amazing copiers.

134
00:05:05,574 --> 00:05:08,339
In experiments, young children copy every

135
00:05:08,339 --> 00:05:11,104
step an adult shows them, even steps that

136
00:05:11,104 --> 00:05:12,676
are obviously useless,

137
00:05:13,326 --> 00:05:15,229
while chimpanzees skip straight to what

138
00:05:15,229 --> 00:05:15,637
works.

139
00:05:16,287 --> 00:05:18,645
Psychologists call it overimitation.

140
00:05:19,295 --> 00:05:21,479
It sounds silly. But it's exactly what

141
00:05:21,479 --> 00:05:23,396
would have saved Burke and Wills:

142
00:05:24,046 --> 00:05:26,461
when you don't know why a step is there,

143
00:05:26,461 --> 00:05:28,608
copying it anyway can keep you alive.

144
00:05:29,258 --> 00:05:31,195
There's one more ingredient:

145
00:05:31,195 --> 00:05:33,819
we cooperate on a scale no other mammal

146
00:05:33,819 --> 00:05:34,319
does.

147
00:05:34,969 --> 00:05:37,501
Psychologist Michael Tomasello found

148
00:05:37,501 --> 00:05:39,971
that even toddlers spontaneously help

149
00:05:39,971 --> 00:05:40,773
strangers,

150
00:05:41,423 --> 00:05:43,687
point things out to share attention, and

151
00:05:43,687 --> 00:05:45,160
work toward shared goals.

152
00:05:45,810 --> 00:05:47,159
Great apes rarely do.

153
00:05:47,809 --> 00:05:51,103
Anthropologist Robin Dunbar suggested we

154
00:05:51,103 --> 00:05:54,031
can keep track of around 150 personal

155
00:05:54,031 --> 00:05:55,276
relationships.

156
00:05:55,926 --> 00:05:58,341
But humans found ways to cooperate far

157
00:05:58,341 --> 00:05:59,225
beyond that:

158
00:05:59,875 --> 00:06:02,565
through stories, rules, gods and money,

159
00:06:02,565 --> 00:06:05,071
things that exist because we all agree

160
00:06:05,071 --> 00:06:05,743
they do.

161
00:06:06,393 --> 00:06:09,103
One of the oldest monuments we know,

162
00:06:09,103 --> 00:06:12,230
Göbekli Tepe in southeastern Turkey, was

163
00:06:12,230 --> 00:06:14,522
built around 11,000 years ago,

164
00:06:15,172 --> 00:06:17,730
by people who, as far as we know, had not

165
00:06:17,730 --> 00:06:19,067
yet started farming.

166
00:06:19,717 --> 00:06:22,343
Huge carved pillars, raised by hundreds of

167
00:06:22,343 --> 00:06:23,860
hands working together.

168
00:06:24,510 --> 00:06:26,262
Cooperation came first.

169
00:06:26,262 --> 00:06:27,947
Civilization followed.

170
00:06:28,597 --> 00:06:30,681
And that's how a weak, slow ape took over

171
00:06:30,681 --> 00:06:31,249
a planet.

172
00:06:31,899 --> 00:06:34,722
When humans arrived in Australia and the

173
00:06:34,722 --> 00:06:37,546
Americas, many giant animals, like giant

174
00:06:37,546 --> 00:06:40,237
kangaroos, mammoths and ground sloths,

175
00:06:40,237 --> 00:06:41,222
disappeared.

176
00:06:41,872 --> 00:06:44,241
Scientists still debate how much was

177
00:06:44,241 --> 00:06:46,913
hunting and how much was climate, but the

178
00:06:46,913 --> 00:06:48,613
timing is hard to ignore.

179
00:06:49,263 --> 00:06:52,293
Today, if you weighed every wild mammal on

180
00:06:52,293 --> 00:06:55,256
Earth, they would make up only about 4%

181
00:06:55,256 --> 00:06:56,940
of all mammal biomass.

182
00:06:57,590 --> 00:06:59,343
Humans are about a third.

183
00:06:59,343 --> 00:07:02,036
Our livestock — cows, pigs, sheep — make

184
00:07:02,036 --> 00:07:03,477
up most of the rest.

185
00:07:04,127 --> 00:07:06,057
We may not be at the top of the food

186
00:07:06,057 --> 00:07:06,502
chain.

187
00:07:07,152 --> 00:07:09,576
But we've become something no species has

188
00:07:09,576 --> 00:07:11,946
been before: the force that shapes the

189
00:07:11,946 --> 00:07:12,717
food chain.

190
00:07:13,367 --> 00:07:15,905
Try this. Look around you right now,

191
00:07:16,555 --> 00:07:19,031
and count how many things you can see that

192
00:07:19,031 --> 00:07:21,288
you could make entirely by yourself,

193
00:07:21,288 --> 00:07:22,168
from scratch.

194
00:07:22,818 --> 00:07:24,314
The chair? The glass?

195
00:07:24,314 --> 00:07:26,184
The clothes you're wearing?

196
00:07:26,184 --> 00:07:28,553
The device you're watching this on?

197
00:07:29,203 --> 00:07:30,270
Probably zero.

198
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:32,479
And that's not a weakness.

199
00:07:32,479 --> 00:07:33,769
It's the whole point.

200
00:07:34,419 --> 00:07:36,459
You're surrounded by the knowledge of

201
00:07:36,459 --> 00:07:38,500
millions of people you'll never meet.

202
00:07:39,150 --> 00:07:41,235
We didn't win because we were the

203
00:07:41,235 --> 00:07:43,785
strongest, or even because each of us was

204
00:07:43,785 --> 00:07:44,712
the smartest.

205
00:07:45,362 --> 00:07:47,774
We won because we learned to share fire,

206
00:07:47,774 --> 00:07:50,019
share knowledge, and share goals with

207
00:07:50,019 --> 00:07:51,421
people we'd never met.

208
00:07:52,071 --> 00:07:53,285
That's our superpower.

209
00:07:53,935 --> 00:07:56,367
And it comes with a question that no other

210
00:07:56,367 --> 00:07:58,205
species has ever had to answer:

211
00:07:58,855 --> 00:08:00,876
now that we shape the planet, what will we

212
00:08:00,876 --> 00:08:01,594
do with it?

